    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION 1: The Model -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="model">
      <h2>The Two-Sided Syndicated Model</h2>
      <p>BuyerVoice syndicated studies serve two buyer segments from the same underlying research:</p>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="model-card vendor">
          <h3>Vendor Side</h3>
          <p class="model-example">e.g., 3 HR software brands included in a study</p>
          <p>"Here's how 200 HR leaders actually talk about your category, your brand, and your competitors."</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Competitive perception data</li>
            <li>Messaging gap analysis</li>
            <li>Switching drivers and barriers</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="model-note">Vendors pay a premium. This is the primary revenue source.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="model-card buyer">
          <h3>Buyer Side</h3>
          <p class="model-example">e.g., HR leaders evaluating software</p>
          <p>"Here's an independent, interview-based guide to help you make a better purchasing decision."</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Decision criteria from real buyers</li>
            <li>User experience comparisons</li>
            <li>Switching analysis frameworks</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="model-note">Lower-cost add-on, lead gen vehicle, or credibility builder.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Same data, different cuts.</strong> The vendor report emphasizes competitive positioning and messaging effectiveness. The buyer report emphasizes decision criteria and comparison frameworks. This is efficient and commercially smart.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION 2: Competitive Landscape -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="competitive-landscape">
      <h2>Competitive Landscape</h2>

      <h3>How G2 Actually Works</h3>
      <p>G2 is the platform most people compare to when they hear "B2B software research." But it operates very differently from what BuyerVoice is building.</p>

      <div class="info-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Data Collection</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Users write reviews (min ~150 words) for <strong>$25 gift cards</strong></li>
            <li>10,000+ gift cards distributed monthly</li>
            <li>Reviews are self-reported, incentivized, and vendor-prompted</li>
            <li>No structured interview methodology, no follow-up probing</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Revenue (~$100M+ ARR)</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>~85% from vendor subscriptions ($15K-$100K+/year)</li>
            <li>Badge licensing (Leader, High Performer)</li>
            <li>Buyer intent data and market intelligence</li>
            <li>Buyers access G2 for <strong>free</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p><strong>G2 is a review aggregation platform, not a research firm.</strong> No study design, no switching analysis, no marketing science applied to the data. Useful as a comparison tool, but fundamentally different data quality from 200 structured interviews analyzed through a codebook.</p>

      <h3>Full Competitive Map</h3>

      <h4>Tier 1: Research-Based, Sold to Vendors</h4>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Player</th><th>What They Do</th><th>Revenue/Scale</th><th>How They Differ</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Forrester</strong></td>
              <td>Syndicated analyst reports (Wave). Analyst opinion + vendor briefings + customer reference calls.</td>
              <td>$432M (2024), declining 10% YoY</td>
              <td>Analyst-driven, not data-driven. Expensive ($25-40K/year per seat), slow.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Gartner</strong></td>
              <td>Magic Quadrant reports. $30-50K+/year per seat subscriptions.</td>
              <td>$6.5B (2025). ~70% from buyers.</td>
              <td>Same model as Forrester but larger. No systematic buyer interviews.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>IDC</strong></td>
              <td>Market sizing, forecasting, vendor evaluations.</td>
              <td>Private, est. $500M+</td>
              <td>Quantitative market data focus, not qualitative buyer perception.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Wynter</strong></td>
              <td>On-demand B2B research. Panel of 60K+ verified professionals. Surveys and message tests in 12-48 hours.</td>
              <td>Pricing from ~$199/test</td>
              <td>Fast, lightweight, survey-oriented. Not deep interviews. Not syndicated.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h4>Tier 2: Review/Evidence Platforms</h4>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Player</th><th>What They Do</th><th>Scale</th><th>How They Differ</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>G2</strong></td>
              <td>Incentivized review aggregation. Free for buyers.</td>
              <td>2M+ reviews, $100M+ ARR</td>
              <td>Shallow data. No study design. Massive scale but low depth.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>TrustRadius</strong></td>
              <td>Longer-form reviews (~400 words avg). No incentivization.</td>
              <td>~$30K/product/year</td>
              <td>Higher quality than G2 but still self-reported, not structured interviews.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Gartner Peer Insights</strong></td>
              <td>Anonymous verified reviews tied to Magic Quadrant categories.</td>
              <td>780K+ ratings</td>
              <td>Part of Gartner's ecosystem. Not independently purchasable.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>PeerSpot</strong></td>
              <td>Community-driven enterprise tech reviews. Triple-validated.</td>
              <td>749K members, 600-word avg</td>
              <td>Detailed but still user-generated, not researcher-designed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>UserEvidence</strong></td>
              <td>Customer evidence platform for vendors.</td>
              <td>Series A stage</td>
              <td>Vendor marketing tool, not independent research.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h4>Tier 3: Buyer-Side Decision Platforms</h4>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Player</th><th>What They Do</th><th>Scale</th><th>How They Differ</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Gartner Digital Markets</strong></td>
              <td>Capterra, GetApp, Software Advice. Vendors pay for placement.</td>
              <td>100M+ B2B buyers</td>
              <td>Lead generation play, not research.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>CB Insights</strong></td>
              <td>Market intelligence, company data, funding analysis.</td>
              <td>$25-50K/year</td>
              <td>Company-level data, not buyer perception.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>AlphaSense</strong></td>
              <td>AI search across filings, transcripts, research.</td>
              <td>$500M ARR, $4B valuation</td>
              <td>Document search tool, not primary research.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION 3: Differentiation -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="differentiation">
      <h2>Where BuyerVoice Is Different</h2>

      <div class="diff-grid">
        <div class="diff-card">
          <div class="diff-number">1</div>
          <h4>Depth + Scale in the Same Product</h4>
          <p>G2 has scale (millions of reviews) but shallow data. Forrester has depth (analyst judgment) but tiny sample sizes. BuyerVoice produces 200 structured interviews per study: a genuine middle ground nobody occupies.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="diff-card">
          <div class="diff-number">2</div>
          <h4>Researcher-Designed Methodology</h4>
          <p>Every other platform either lets users self-report (G2, TrustRadius) or relies on analyst opinion (Gartner, Forrester). BuyerVoice applies an actual research design: controlled sample, structured interview guide, switching analysis, codebook-based thematic analysis, and segmentation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="diff-card">
          <div class="diff-number">3</div>
          <h4>Switching Analysis as Core Method</h4>
          <p>Nobody in this landscape systematically studies WHY buyers switched, considered switching, or rejected alternatives. G2 reviews are from current users. BuyerVoice captures the decision itself, which is what vendors need to improve win rates.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="diff-card">
          <div class="diff-number">4</div>
          <h4>Two-Sided Monetization</h4>
          <p>Vendor report: competitive positioning, perception gaps, messaging effectiveness. Buyer report: decision criteria, real user experiences, comparison frameworks. Same data, different cuts.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="diff-card">
          <div class="diff-number">5</div>
          <h4>No Vendor Influence on the Research</h4>
          <p>G2 reviews are vendor-prompted. Gartner evaluations involve vendor briefings and vendor-selected references. BuyerVoice recruits independently and applies a consistent methodology. The independence is a selling point to both sides.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Key Risks</h3>
      <div class="risk-grid">
        <div class="risk-card">
          <h4>Credibility at Launch</h4>
          <p>Gartner, Forrester, and G2 have brand recognition. The first 2-3 studies may need aggressive pricing or partial self-funding to build proof.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="risk-card">
          <h4>Sample Recruitment Cost</h4>
          <p>200 qualified professionals per study is a real operational cost. AI moderation helps with scale but does not eliminate the cost of finding and incentivizing qualified participants.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="risk-card">
          <h4>Syndicated vs Custom Sell</h4>
          <p>Some vendors will love benchmarking; others will want exclusivity. Product packaging needs to be clear about what is shared vs proprietary.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="risk-card">
          <h4>Buyer-Side Distribution</h4>
          <p>G2 wins on the buyer side because it is where people already go. The buyer report needs a distribution strategy (SEO, partnerships, gated content).</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION 4: B2C Assessment -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="b2c-assessment">
      <h2>B2C Assessment</h2>

      <div class="callout callout-warning">
        <strong>Not recommended.</strong> The syndicated model depends on four structural conditions that hold in B2B but break in B2C.
      </div>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Condition</th><th>B2B Software</th><th>B2C Consumer</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Buyers will pay for research</td>
              <td class="cell-pass">Yes. VPs evaluating $50K-$500K platforms pay $500-$2,000 for a report.</td>
              <td class="cell-fail">No. Consumers expect comparison content for free (Wirecutter, YouTube, Reddit).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Vendors have high WTP and few alternatives</td>
              <td class="cell-pass">Yes. G2 is shallow, Gartner is expensive, nobody does interviews at scale.</td>
              <td class="cell-fail">No. JD Power, NielsenIQ ($4.5B), Kantar (~$4B), Ipsos already serve this.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Interview depth produces rich data</td>
              <td class="cell-pass">Yes. B2B decisions are complex, multi-stakeholder, high-stakes.</td>
              <td class="cell-fail">Diminished. Consumer decisions are simpler. Depth advantage shrinks.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Clear competitive gap</td>
              <td class="cell-pass">Yes. Nobody does structured interviews at scale for B2B software.</td>
              <td class="cell-fail">No. JD Power has done syndicated consumer research for decades.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Keep the focus on B2B software categories where the model's structural advantages are strongest.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION 5: Panel Providers -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="panel-providers">
      <h2>Panel Providers</h2>

      <p>Each syndicated study requires ~200 participants from a specific B2B persona. BuyerVoice is building a custom AI moderator platform. From the panel's perspective, participants click a link, complete the interview asynchronously, and get paid. This qualifies as <strong>unmoderated</strong> research.</p>

      <h3>Tier 1: Recommended</h3>

      <div class="provider-card primary">
        <div class="provider-header">
          <h4>Respondent.io</h4>
          <span class="badge badge-primary">Primary Recommendation</span>
        </div>
        <div class="provider-grid">
          <div class="provider-stat">
            <span class="stat-label">B2B Panel Size</span>
            <span class="stat-value">4M+ participants</span>
          </div>
          <div class="provider-stat">
            <span class="stat-label">B2B Unmoderated (PAYG)</span>
            <span class="stat-value">$40/session</span>
          </div>
          <div class="provider-stat">
            <span class="stat-label">B2B Unmoderated (Bundle)</span>
            <span class="stat-value">$34/session</span>
          </div>
          <div class="provider-stat">
            <span class="stat-label">Fraud Rate</span>
            <span class="stat-value">&lt;1%</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="provider-details">
          <p><strong>Quality checks:</strong> Phone verification, IP monitoring, B2B email domain validation (re-verified every 90 days), profile locks, screener consistency checks, bank-grade ID verification for high-risk applicants, human fraud review team.</p>
          <p><strong>Incentives:</strong> Built-in Tremendous integration. Participants choose their reward (Visa prepaid, Amazon, etc.). No separate Tremendous account needed.</p>
          <p><strong>Integration:</strong> Supports external study links via Completion Confirmation URL. Custom platforms redirect participants back on completion.</p>
          <p><strong>API:</strong> Full API for platform partners: participant status updates, messaging, webhooks, payout management.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="provider-card secondary">
        <div class="provider-header">
          <h4>User Interviews</h4>
          <span class="badge badge-secondary">Secondary</span>
        </div>
        <div class="provider-grid">
          <div class="provider-stat">
            <span class="stat-label">B2B Panel Size</span>
            <span class="stat-value">6M+ participants</span>
          </div>
          <div class="provider-stat">
            <span class="stat-label">B2B Base Pricing</span>
            <span class="stat-value">$72-$98/session</span>
          </div>
          <div class="provider-stat">
            <span class="stat-label">Advanced B2B Targeting</span>
            <span class="stat-value">+$20-$49/session</span>
          </div>
          <div class="provider-stat">
            <span class="stat-label">Verification</span>
            <span class="stat-value">LinkedIn + corp email</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="provider-details">
          <p><strong>When to use:</strong> If Respondent's quality proves insufficient for sensitive audiences (C-suite targets). Stronger verification but roughly 1.5-1.7x the cost.</p>
          <p><strong>Incentives:</strong> Also uses Tremendous. Auto-distribute option with 3% processing fee.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Tier 2: Viable Alternatives</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Panel</th><th>Pricing</th><th>Quality</th><th>Best For</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Prolific</strong></td>
              <td>20K+ professionals</td>
              <td>Reward + 42.8% fee</td>
              <td>50+ checks, but self-reported titles</td>
              <td>General professional audiences, less niche targets</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>NewtonX</strong></td>
              <td>Scans 1.1B+ (custom-recruits)</td>
              <td>~$165/survey, $400-$700/IDI</td>
              <td>Best in class. 100% two-step verified.</td>
              <td>Premium custom engagements, not syndicated</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Tier 3: Enterprise (Quote-Based)</h3>
      <div class="tier3-list">
        <p><strong>Dynata:</strong> Largest global panel. B2B validation against third-party databases. Custom incidence-based pricing. Enterprise-oriented.</p>
        <p><strong>Sago (formerly Schlesinger Group):</strong> 55 years in the industry. Strong qualitative recruitment. Full-service. Quote-based.</p>
        <p><strong>InnovateMR (+ Ivy Exec):</strong> ISO-certified. 50+ quality checks. LinkedIn API + resume verification. Good for C-suite audiences.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION 6: Cost Estimates -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="cost-estimates">
      <h2>Cost Estimates</h2>
      <p>Based on 200 B2B participants, 20-minute unmoderated study, US IT managers/directors, using Respondent bundle rate ($34/session).</p>

      <h3>How Respondent Pricing Works</h3>
      <div class="info-grid three-col">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Recruiting Fee</h4>
          <p>Flat per-session charge. Affected by: B2B vs B2C, moderated vs unmoderated, plan tier.</p>
          <p><strong>NOT affected by:</strong> duration, video requirements, audience specificity.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Incentive</h4>
          <p>Set by researcher. One flat amount per project. Affected by: seniority, duration, recording type, fill speed needed.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Tremendous Fee</h4>
          <p>5% deducted from the participant's incentive. Paid by participant, not researcher.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Incentive Levels by Scenario</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Target Audience</th><th>Est. Hourly Rate</th><th>Audio-Only (20 min)</th><th>Audio + Video (20 min)</th><th>Video Premium</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>IT Managers</td>
              <td>$55-$65/hr</td>
              <td>$45-$55</td>
              <td>$55-$65</td>
              <td>+$10/participant</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>IT Directors</td>
              <td>$75-$90/hr</td>
              <td>$60-$75</td>
              <td>$75-$90</td>
              <td>+$15/participant</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mixed</td>
              <td></td>
              <td>$50-$65</td>
              <td>$65-$80</td>
              <td>+$10-15/participant</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Full Cost Scenarios</h3>

      <div class="scenario-grid">
        <div class="scenario-card">
          <div class="scenario-label">Scenario A</div>
          <h4>Audio-only, flat incentive</h4>
          <div class="scenario-details">
            <div class="scenario-line"><span>Recruiting fee</span><span>200 &times; $34 = $6,800</span></div>
            <div class="scenario-line"><span>Incentives</span><span>200 &times; $55 = $11,000</span></div>
            <div class="scenario-total"><span>Total</span><span>$17,800</span></div>
            <p class="scenario-per">$89 per participant</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="scenario-card">
          <div class="scenario-label">Scenario B</div>
          <h4>Audio + Video, flat incentive</h4>
          <div class="scenario-details">
            <div class="scenario-line"><span>Recruiting fee</span><span>200 &times; $34 = $6,800</span></div>
            <div class="scenario-line"><span>Incentives</span><span>200 &times; $70 = $14,000</span></div>
            <div class="scenario-total"><span>Total</span><span>$20,800</span></div>
            <p class="scenario-per">$104 per participant</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="scenario-card">
          <div class="scenario-label">Scenario C</div>
          <h4>Audio-only, split by seniority</h4>
          <div class="scenario-details">
            <div class="scenario-line"><span>100 Managers</span><span>$3,400 + $4,500 = $7,900</span></div>
            <div class="scenario-line"><span>100 Directors</span><span>$3,400 + $6,500 = $9,900</span></div>
            <div class="scenario-total"><span>Total</span><span>$17,800</span></div>
            <p class="scenario-per">$89 per participant</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="scenario-card">
          <div class="scenario-label">Scenario D</div>
          <h4>Audio + Video, split by seniority</h4>
          <div class="scenario-details">
            <div class="scenario-line"><span>100 Managers</span><span>$3,400 + $5,500 = $8,900</span></div>
            <div class="scenario-line"><span>100 Directors</span><span>$3,400 + $8,000 = $11,400</span></div>
            <div class="scenario-total"><span>Total</span><span>$20,300</span></div>
            <p class="scenario-per">$102 per participant</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Incremental cost of adding video: ~$2,500-$3,000 per study.</strong> Entirely driven by higher incentives. Zero change in Respondent's recruiting fees. Recommendation: start with audio-only and add video if quality issues emerge.
      </div>

      <h3>Incentive Structure: How It Works</h3>
      <p>You set <strong>one flat incentive amount per project.</strong> All participants who complete that project receive the same amount. To set different incentives by seniority, run two separate Respondent projects (e.g., "IT Managers" at $50 and "IT Directors" at $75). This gives precise quotas per seniority level and avoids overpaying managers or underpaying directors.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION 7: Study Workflow -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="study-workflow">
      <h2>Study Workflow</h2>
      <p>BuyerVoice is building a custom AI moderator application (not a third-party interview tool). This gives full control over the interview experience, recording requirements, screener logic, transcript format, and data pipeline.</p>
      <p><strong>Platform architecture (decided August 21, 2026):</strong> v1 of the custom platform is a half-duplex, turn-based moderator (question, spoken answer, transcription, next question), not a full-duplex conversational voice agent. That choice keeps the moderator neutral, prevents mid-answer cut-offs, and produces one response per question for the coding pipeline. If later studies show participants want a more conversational moderator, LiveKit Agents is the upgrade path: the moderator logic carries over and only the transport and turn-taking layer changes, at about $50 more per 200-participant study. Details are in the PRD (Section 5.4) and the cost estimate in the <code>AI-moderator-product</code> folder of the repo.</p>

      <h3>End-to-End Flow</h3>

      <div class="workflow">
        <div class="workflow-phase">
          <h4>Setup Phase</h4>
          <div class="workflow-steps">
            <div class="workflow-step">
              <span class="step-num">1</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Create Respondent project</strong>
                <p>Select "Unmoderated" methodology. Write 2-20 screener questions. Set incentive. Provide BuyerVoice interview URL with dynamic participant ID parameter. Configure Completion Confirmation URL.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="workflow-step">
              <span class="step-num">2</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Respondent displays study to panelists</strong>
                <p>Matching B2B panelists see the description, duration, and incentive amount. Interested panelists fill out screener questions (unpaid).</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="workflow-step">
              <span class="step-num">3</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Review and invite</strong>
                <p>Review screener responses in the dashboard (or via API). Invite qualified participants. Disqualify unqualified (no charge, no notification).</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="workflow-phase">
          <h4>Interview Phase</h4>
          <div class="workflow-steps">
            <div class="workflow-step">
              <span class="step-num">4</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Participant clicks study link</strong>
                <p>URL includes their Respondent ID as a parameter:<br />
                <code>https://interview.buyervoice.ai/study/XYZ?rid=RESPONDENT_ID</code></p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="workflow-step">
              <span class="step-num">5</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Platform-side screening (optional)</strong>
                <p>Verify domain knowledge. If they fail, session ends, no Completion URL triggered, no charge.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="workflow-step">
              <span class="step-num">6</span>
              <div>
                <strong>AI-moderated interview</strong>
                <p>Audio or audio+video recording. Platform transcribes and saves to JSON with the Respondent participant ID.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="workflow-step">
              <span class="step-num">7</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Completion redirect</strong>
                <p>BuyerVoice platform redirects to Respondent's Completion Confirmation URL. Auto-marks participant as "completed."</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="workflow-phase">
          <h4>Data Merge and Quality Phase</h4>
          <div class="workflow-steps">
            <div class="workflow-step">
              <span class="step-num">8</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Merge screener + interview data</strong>
                <p>Pull screener answers from Respondent (CSV or API). Match to interview JSON using participant ID. One merged JSON per participant.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="workflow-step">
              <span class="step-num">9</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Quality agent review</strong>
                <p>Checks response length, domain knowledge, consistency. Flags low-quality participants.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="workflow-step">
              <span class="step-num">10</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Approve or reject</strong>
                <p>Within 48 hours of completion. PASS: confirm, auto-pay fires. FAIL: mark No Show via dashboard or API, no charge.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Participant ID Integration</h3>
      <p>The critical integration point: embedding each participant's Respondent ID into the BuyerVoice interview URL so the two datasets can be matched.</p>
      <div class="code-block">
        <code>https://interview.buyervoice.ai/study/hr-leaders-2026?rid=&#123;RESPONDENT_PARTICIPANT_ID&#125;</code>
      </div>
      <p>When a participant clicks "Start Project" in Respondent, they are redirected to this URL with their unique ID filled in. The BuyerVoice platform reads the <code>rid</code> parameter and stores it alongside interview data.</p>

      <h3>Merged Data Format</h3>
      <p>After merging, each participant produces one JSON file containing screener data, demographics, and the full interview transcript. This feeds directly into the coding pipeline with screener data available as descriptor and criterion variables.</p>
      <div class="code-block">
<pre>&#123;
  "participant_id": "resp_ABC123",
  "study": "hr-leaders-2026",
  "screener": &#123;
    "job_title": "IT Director",
    "company_size": "500-1000 employees",
    "current_platform": "ServiceNow",
    "years_in_role": "5-10"
  &#125;,
  "demographics": &#123;
    "location": "United States",
    "industry": "Financial Services"
  &#125;,
  "interview": &#123;
    "duration_seconds": 1180,
    "recording_type": "audio",
    "quality_status": "approved",
    "transcript": [...]
  &#125;
&#125;</pre>
      </div>

      <h3>Two-Layer Screening</h3>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Layer 1: Respondent Screener</h4>
          <p>2-20 questions on Respondent's platform, answered unpaid. You review and invite. Rejected applicants cost nothing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Layer 2: Platform Screening</h4>
          <p>Additional verification at the start of the BuyerVoice interview. If they fail, no Completion URL triggered, no charge.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION 8: Automated Quality Control -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="quality-control">
      <h2>Automated Quality Control</h2>

      <h3>The Problem</h3>
      <p>The main quality risk is <strong>low-effort participation</strong>: people who technically qualify but rush through the interview giving one-sentence answers to collect their incentive. At 200 participants per study, manually reviewing every transcript is time-consuming.</p>

      <h3>Respondent API Capabilities</h3>
      <div class="info-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>What the API Supports</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Participant status updates</li>
            <li>Messaging API (required before marking No Show)</li>
            <li>Webhooks for real-time notifications</li>
            <li>Payout management</li>
            <li>Screener question and response retrieval</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Access Requirement</h4>
          <p>API is designed for <strong>platform partners</strong>, not individual researchers. BuyerVoice should apply as a research platform. Benefits: programmatic management, wholesale rates, webhook integration. Timeline: ~2 weeks.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Automated Pipeline</h3>
      <div class="pipeline">
        <div class="pipeline-step">
          <div class="pipeline-icon">1</div>
          <p>Participant completes interview on BuyerVoice platform, redirected to Completion URL</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pipeline-arrow">&darr;</div>
        <div class="pipeline-step">
          <div class="pipeline-icon">2</div>
          <p>Respondent webhook fires. System pulls transcript and merges with screener data.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pipeline-arrow">&darr;</div>
        <div class="pipeline-step">
          <div class="pipeline-icon">3</div>
          <p><strong>Quality agent checks:</strong> response length, domain knowledge, consistency, AI-generated filler, screener match</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pipeline-arrow">&darr;</div>
        <div class="pipeline-step pass">
          <div class="pipeline-icon">&#10003;</div>
          <p><strong>PASS:</strong> Mark completed via API. Incentive payout triggered.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pipeline-step fail">
          <div class="pipeline-icon">&#10007;</div>
          <p><strong>FAIL:</strong> API sends message explaining why. Status updated to No Show. No charge, no incentive.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Without API Access (Fallback)</h3>
      <ol class="fallback-list">
        <li>Run quality agent locally against all transcripts after the study</li>
        <li>Agent produces a "reject list" with participant IDs and reasons</li>
        <li>Manually process rejections in Respondent dashboard (~30 minutes per study)</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Payment Options for Problem Participants</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Action</th><th>Cost to BuyerVoice</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Fake credentials or zero-effort responses</td>
              <td>Mark as <strong>No Show</strong></td>
              <td>No recruiting fee, no incentive</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Legitimate but mediocre effort</td>
              <td><strong>Partial incentive</strong> (contact Respondent support)</td>
              <td>Recruiting fee applies, reduced incentive</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Minor issues but usable data</td>
              <td><strong>Full payment</strong></td>
              <td>Full recruiting fee + full incentive</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Critical timing:</strong> Auto-pay fires 48 hours after completion. Researcher is billed at 72 hours. Recommendation: turn off auto-pay for syndicated studies and manually approve after quality review, or run quality checks within the 48-hour window.
      </div>
    </section>

