        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Recommended Study Sequence</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Study</th><th>Category</th><th>Rationale</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>HRIS / Core HR</td><td>Lowest risk: accessible persona, existing experience, high vendor density, strong investor interest</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>CRM</td><td>Largest vendor market, highest buyer report demand</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Project Mgmt or Marketing Automation</td><td>Choose based on sales signal from Study 1-2</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>Combined HR Tech (HRIS + ATS)</td><td>Natural expansion of Study 1; same persona, broader coverage</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Revenue Impact of Category Choice</h3>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Tier 1 Category (e.g., HRIS)</h4>
          <p>3 vendor reports ($60K) + 12 buyer reports ($36K) + 2 investor reports ($24K) + add-ons ($10K) = <strong>$130,000 revenue</strong> on $27K cost.</p>
          <p class="model-note"><strong>382% ROI</strong></p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Tier 3 Category (e.g., Accounting)</h4>
          <p>1 vendor report ($20K) + 5 buyer reports ($15K) + 1 investor report ($12K) = <strong>$47,000-$60,000 revenue</strong> on $27K cost.</p>
          <p class="model-note">Still profitable, but dramatically lower upside.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION 11: Interview Participant Profiles -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="participant-profiles">
      <h2>Interview Participant Profiles</h2>

      <p>For each study, participants must simultaneously be: (a) the customer vendors are selling to, (b) a mirror of the buyer-report reader, and (c) someone with opinions on multiple platforms. Company size is <strong>50-1,000 employees</strong> across all categories.</p>

      <h3>Why 50-1,000 (Not 50-500)</h3>
      <div class="info-grid three-col">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>50-199: "First Real Purchase"</h4>
          <p>Graduating from spreadsheets to professional tools. Highest evaluation volume, most vendors competing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>200-499: "Upgrade or Switch"</h4>
          <p>Outgrowing first tool. Most active competitive battles happen here.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>500-1,000: "Enterprise-Adjacent"</h4>
          <p>Most sophisticated mid-market buyers. Vendors here have the largest marketing budgets and are most likely to buy a $20K+ vendor report.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Standard Quotas (200 Participants)</h3>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <h4>Company Size</h4>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Size Band</th><th>Target</th><th>%</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>50-199 employees</td><td>80</td><td>40%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>200-499 employees</td><td>70</td><td>35%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>500-1,000 employees</td><td>50</td><td>25%</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>Seniority</h4>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Target</th><th>%</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Manager</td><td>60</td><td>30%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Director</td><td>90</td><td>45%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>VP+</td><td>50</td><td>25%</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Universal qualification:</strong> Must have evaluated, selected, or been actively involved in the purchase decision for the relevant category in the past 18 months. Each participant expected to discuss ~3 platforms they've evaluated or used.
      </div>

      <h3>Tier 1: Detailed Profiles</h3>

      <div class="profile-card">
        <div class="profile-header">
          <h4>Study 1: HRIS / Core HR</h4>
          <span class="badge badge-primary">Recommended First</span>
        </div>
        <div class="profile-body">
          <div class="two-col">
            <div>
              <strong>Target Titles (priority order)</strong>
              <ol>
                <li>Director of People / HR / People Operations</li>
                <li>VP of People / HR / Human Resources</li>
                <li>Head of People / HR</li>
                <li>HR Manager (at 50-200 cos, often the senior HR person)</li>
                <li>Director/Manager of HR Operations or HRIS</li>
              </ol>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong>Do NOT include</strong>
              <ul>
                <li>CHRO (rare in mid-market, more enterprise)</li>
                <li>HR Coordinators / Specialists (too junior)</li>
                <li>IT leaders (don't drive HRIS selection in mid-market)</li>
              </ul>
              <strong>Quotas</strong>
              <p>Standard size (40/35/25) and seniority (30/45/25). No industry quotas needed.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p><strong>Expected vendor coverage (15+ mentions):</strong> Rippling, BambooHR, Gusto, Paycor, Paylocity, Paycom, HiBob. <strong>Moderate (5-15):</strong> Deel, Namely, JustWorks, TriNet, ADP Workforce Now.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="profile-card">
        <div class="profile-header">
          <h4>Study 2: CRM</h4>
        </div>
        <div class="profile-body">
          <div class="two-col">
            <div>
              <strong>Target Titles</strong>
              <ol>
                <li>Director of Sales / Sales Director</li>
                <li>VP of Sales</li>
                <li>Head of Sales / Head of Revenue</li>
                <li>Director/Manager of Sales Operations</li>
                <li>Director/Manager of Revenue Operations</li>
              </ol>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong>Additional filter:</strong> B2B companies only (or B2B-dominant revenue).
              <p><strong>Quotas:</strong> Standard size and seniority. No department quotas needed.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p><strong>Expected coverage:</strong> HubSpot CRM, Salesforce, Pipedrive, Zoho CRM, Freshsales, Monday CRM, Close, Copper, Insightly.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="profile-card">
        <div class="profile-header">
          <h4>Study 3: Project Management</h4>
          <span class="badge badge-secondary">Requires Department Quotas</span>
        </div>
        <div class="profile-body">
          <p><strong>Critical difference:</strong> PM tools are bought by different departments (Operations, Engineering, Product, Marketing). Without department quotas, you risk a sample skewed toward one tool cluster.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Department</th><th>Target</th><th>%</th><th>Tools Covered</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Operations / PMO</td><td>70</td><td>35%</td><td>Monday, Asana, Smartsheet, Wrike</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Engineering / Product</td><td>60</td><td>30%</td><td>Jira, Linear, Shortcut, Height</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Marketing / Sales / Other</td><td>40</td><td>20%</td><td>Monday, Asana, ClickUp, Notion</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Executive (COO, VP)</td><td>30</td><td>15%</td><td>Strategic-level decisions</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Tier 2-3: Summary Profiles</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Category</th><th>Primary Persona</th><th>Seniority Sweet Spot</th><th>Special Quotas</th><th>Key Vendors</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Marketing Automation</td>
              <td>Marketing leaders (B2B only)</td>
              <td>Director (45%)</td>
              <td>None</td>
              <td>HubSpot, ActiveCampaign, Mailchimp, Brevo, Klaviyo</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Help Desk</td>
              <td>Support / CS leaders</td>
              <td>Director (40%)</td>
              <td>None</td>
              <td>Zendesk, Freshdesk, Intercom, Help Scout, Front</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>ATS / Recruiting</td>
              <td>TA / HR leaders</td>
              <td>Director (40%)</td>
              <td>None; pairs with HRIS</td>
              <td>Greenhouse, Lever, Ashby, Workable, JazzHR</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Sales Engagement</td>
              <td>Sales / RevOps (B2B only)</td>
              <td>Director (40%)</td>
              <td>Overlaps with CRM</td>
              <td>Outreach, SalesLoft, Apollo, Gong</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Accounting / ERP</td>
              <td>Finance leaders</td>
              <td>Director/VP (skew senior)</td>
              <td>Size quotas shifted to 200+</td>
              <td>NetSuite, Sage Intacct, Xero, Acumatica</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION 12: Open Questions -->
    <!-- ============================================ -->
    <section id="strategic-questions">
      <h2>Open Questions</h2>
      <div class="questions-list">
        <div class="question-item">
          <span class="question-num">1</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Which category first?</strong>
            <p>The HR software study is a natural pilot. What are the next 2-3 categories with enough vendor concentration and buyer demand?</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="question-item">
          <span class="question-num">2</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Minimum viable vendor commitment</strong>
            <p>Do all vendors need to commit before running the study, or self-fund first and sell access after?</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="question-item">
          <span class="question-num">3</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Buyer-side product format</strong>
            <p>Full downloadable report? Interactive web page? Gated or ungated? This affects both revenue and distribution.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="question-item">
          <span class="question-num">4</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Recurring cadence</strong>
            <p>Syndicated reports gain value when repeated (annual or semi-annual). Vendors want to track perception over time.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="question-item">
          <span class="question-num">5</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Respondent API partner access</strong>
            <p>Apply early so the automated quality pipeline is ready before the first full syndicated study.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="question-item">
          <span class="question-num">6</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Pilot study</strong>
            <p>Run 10-20 participants on Respondent to validate fill rate, data quality, and rejection process before committing to 200.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</InternalLayout>

